199. Binary Tree Right Side View

LeetCode - Binary Tree Right Side View

My Approach

When I first looked at this problem, the obvious approach was level order traversal using a queue.

Since the answer needs one node from every level, I could process the tree level by level and take the rightmost node.

But while thinking about it, I realized that I could do the same thing using DFS.

The important part was not just recursion — it was keeping track of:

What level am I currently at?
Have I already added a node for this level?

I used a variable called visited to keep track of the levels that had already been added.

if(visited < level){
    ll.add(root.val);
    visited++;
}

Initially:

visited = -1
level = 0

So the root gets added.

Then I deliberately traverse:

view(root.right, level + 1);
view(root.left, level + 1);

Because the right subtree is always visited first, the first node I encounter at a new level is the node that would be visible from the right side.

So I only add the first node encountered at each level.

The Interesting Part

The main realization for me was that this is basically doing what a level-order traversal does, but without explicitly maintaining a queue.

I am dynamically keeping track of two things:

level   → where I currently am
visited → how many levels have already been answered

So when:

visited < level

it means:

"I have reached a level whose answer hasn't been recorded yet."

Since I go right first, that first node becomes the answer for that level.

Why Right First Matters

Consider:

        1
       / \
      2   3
         / \
        4   5

The traversal order is roughly:

1
→ 3
→ 5
→ 4
→ 2

The first node encountered at each level is:

Level 0 → 1
Level 1 → 3
Level 2 → 5

Therefore:

[1, 3, 5]

is the right side view.

Main Learning

The biggest learning from this problem was not the recursion itself.

It was realizing that traversal order can replace an explicit data structure.

I initially thought:

"This looks like a queue + level order problem."

But then I realized:

"If I visit the right side first and remember which levels I've already handled, I don't need the queue."

That idea of combining traversal order + a small piece of state is what I found most interesting about this problem.

Complexity
Time: O(n) — every node is visited once.
Space: O(h) — recursion stack, where h is the height of the tree.